PostgreSQL 19rc1: BuildTupleHashTable caps a hash table's initial size at hash_mem again (upstream BUG #19759).

Upstream commit 8f29467c removed BuildTupleHashTable's cap of a table's initial bucket count at hash_mem, leaving that
to the planner. A recursive UNION has no unhashed plan and sizes its table from the planner's worst-case estimate, and a
hashed SetOp can be chosen over a table the planner expects to exceed hash_mem, so ExecutorStart zeroes up to 64 GB of
buckets, plain EXPLAIN included, without checking for interrupts. Reproduction (19rc1 23 s, patched 1.6 ms):

	EXPLAIN WITH RECURSIVE r(n) AS (SELECT g FROM generate_series(1, 1000000000) AS g UNION SELECT n FROM r WHERE false)
	SELECT 1 FROM r LIMIT 1;

This is upstream's fix, unchanged: REL_19_STABLE 747dbf30d1 ("Reintroduce hash_mem cap on buckets in
BuildTupleHashTable", David Rowley) with its indentation fix 6bd4b59a9f (master 34d52e6d3d and 227de22095), discussed at
https://postgr.es/m/19759-acd3f33402e54f36@postgresql.org. It restores PostgreSQL 18's cap for every caller. 19rc2
includes it, so the RC2 repin drops this patch.

--- a/src/backend/executor/execGrouping.c
+++ b/src/backend/executor/execGrouping.c
@@ -203,18 +203,37 @@ BuildTupleHashTable(PlanState *parent,
 
 	/*
 	 * tuplehash_create requires a uint32 element count, so we had better
-	 * clamp the given nelements to fit in that.  As long as we have to do
-	 * that, we might as well protect against completely insane input like
-	 * zero or NaN.  But it is not our job here to enforce issues like staying
-	 * within hash_mem: the caller should have done that, and we don't have
-	 * enough info to second-guess.
+	 * clamp the given nelements to fit in that.  We'd also better do
+	 * something sane when faced with a zero or NaN estimate.  There are
+	 * various reasons why the planner might not be able to provide a good
+	 * estimate here; for example, recursive CTEs don't base their estimate on
+	 * anything reliable and can yield some rather large estimates.  Here we
+	 * cap nbuckets to hash_mem to help prevent insane memory allocations. The
+	 * hash table can still grow when the bucket count is insufficient. That
+	 * will make the query a little slower, but that's better than outright
+	 * failing due to OOM if we were to request an insane bucket count.
+	 * Queries being slightly slower with smaller work_mem values is also
+	 * aligned to user expectations.  We purposefully don't include the tuple
+	 * width in this calculation, as that would make the cap depend on planner
+	 * estimates for the tuple width, which could also be unreliable.
 	 */
 	if (isnan(nelements) || nelements <= 0)
 		nbuckets = 1;
-	else if (nelements >= PG_UINT32_MAX)
-		nbuckets = PG_UINT32_MAX;
 	else
-		nbuckets = (uint32) nelements;
+	{
+		size_t		entrysize;
+		size_t		hash_mem_limit;
+
+		if (nelements >= PG_UINT32_MAX)
+			nbuckets = PG_UINT32_MAX;
+		else
+			nbuckets = (uint32) nelements;
+
+		entrysize = sizeof(TupleHashEntryData) + additionalsize;
+		hash_mem_limit = get_hash_memory_limit() / entrysize;
+		if (nbuckets > hash_mem_limit)
+			nbuckets = hash_mem_limit;
+	}
 
 	/* tuplescxt must be separate, else ResetTupleHashTable breaks things */
 	Assert(metacxt != tuplescxt);
